WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.572
Now we move into the guided Python lab.

00:00:02.572 --> 00:00:04.842
The code is intentionally short.

00:00:04.842 --> 00:00:11.717
We will create a small dataframe, inspect it, assign meaningful dtypes, encode categories,

00:00:11.717 --> 00:00:15.507
scale selected numeric features, flatten JSON, and add basic quality checks.

00:00:15.507 --> 00:00:22.327
The most important rule is simple: inspect and understand first, transform second.

00:00:22.327 --> 00:00:27.677
Keep a real project in mind as we work through this section—for example,

00:00:27.677 --> 00:00:31.432
an e-commerce team preparing customer, purchase, and web-event data.

00:00:31.432 --> 00:00:38.732
The technical feature matters because it changes what questions we can answer reliably and how easily another

00:00:38.732 --> 00:00:42.217
analyst can understand our work.

00:00:42.217 --> 00:00:48.732
As you listen, try to connect each SQL or data concept to a decision you might need to

00:00:48.732 --> 00:00:52.207
defend later: what the data means, what one row represents, what can be missing,

00:00:52.207 --> 00:00:56.413
and what assumptions the transformation introduces.
